<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    RDR-192 Step 7 (bead nexus-wbfpw.15): reapable(c), the ONE engine
    predicate for "this chunk is garbage collection's to take", as an
    inlinable SQL function, and the orphaning record that gives its grace
    window the meaning RDR-192 needs. Its first consumers are Step 8's
    gc_quarantine_orphans pair (vectors-022) and the read-only listing route;
    the periodic reaper (nexus-2x9xa) is the third.

    Three changesets, in dependency order (a SQL function body is checked at
    creation, so the table comes first):
      vectors-021-1  nexus.chunk_orphaned_at, the side table the record lives in
      vectors-021-2  nexus.chunk_is_reapable, the predicate
      vectors-021-3  the two triggers on nexus.catalog_document_chunks that write it
    None of the three is in a published engine tag (verified against
    engine-service-v0.1.139 to v0.1.142 and origin/develop), so the file was
    edited in place; only a dev database that ran an earlier draft meets a
    checksum mismatch.

    THE PREDICATE. A chunk row c is reapable when ALL of:

      1. GREATEST(c.last_written_at, the chunk's orphaned_at, if it has one)
         is older than now() - grace (default 30 days). last_written_at, not
         created_at (Sam, 2026-09-29, nexus-wbfpw.43): created_at is write-once,
         so a re-write would otherwise get no fresh grace. last_written_at is
         refreshed by every client write path that re-writes an existing chunk
         (vectors-020's header), including the existence-partition metadata
         refresh (batchUpdateMetadata). orphaned_at is the time the chunk last
         lost an owner row (below). The later of the two is the start of the
         grace, so the grace means "time since the chunk was last written or
         last lost an owner", i.e. how long it has been ownerless.
      2. no catalog_document_chunks row names (tenant, collection, chash) in
         ANY owner state. A tombstoned owner still has a manifest row, so a
         chunk whose only owners are tombstoned is NOT reapable: it is
         purge_trash's, which ages the tombstone, not the chunk.
      3. the chunk's collection is not a quarantine sibling
         (catalog_collections.lifecycle_state = 'quarantine', by state and not
         by name: RDR-204 retired parsing names). A quarantine sibling never has
         manifest rows and its rows take a fresh last_written_at on the move, so
         after 30 days every quarantined chunk would otherwise be reapable and a
         caller that visits quarantine-* collections would bypass
         gc_expire_quarantine's own clock and floors.

    THERE IS NO IN-FLIGHT-INDEX PIN. An earlier draft excluded chunks whose
    metadata catalog_doc_id named a document in index_state 'indexing'. No
    current writer stamps that key (RDR-108 removed doc_id from chunk metadata;
    make_chunk_metadata raises on it), so the pin was empty for every chunk the
    multi-batch writers produce, and its tests passed only on hand-stamped
    fixtures (review T2 nexus/review-wbfpw15-17-code C1 and -critique Issue 1).
    The orphaning record does the job without any metadata key.

    THE ORPHANING RECORD (vectors-021-1 and -3). A multi-batch re-index
    replaces the document's manifest at batch 1, so every old tail chunk is
    ownerless until a later batch re-adds it (RDR-223 Phase 2 gate critique,
    S2); a deleted file's chunks lose their owner when the client purges its
    manifest. In both cases last_written_at is OLD (the chunk was written long
    ago), so a chunk age grace would let the reaper take it at once. Two
    statement-level triggers on nexus.catalog_document_chunks (DELETE, and
    UPDATE) therefore upsert (tenant, collection, chash, orphaned_at = now())
    into nexus.chunk_orphaned_at for every chunk KEY the statement dropped
    from the manifest (the deleted rows' keys; for an UPDATE, the old keys
    minus the new keys, so a row that moves position and chash together still
    records the old chash), in the same transaction as the manifest change.

    WHY A SIDE TABLE AND NOT last_written_at (Sam, 2026-10-01, option b).
    The first version of the orphaning stamp UPDATEd nexus.chunks. Every
    UPDATE of a chunk row writes a new row version, and nexus.chunks carries
    three full HNSW indexes (vector(384), vector(768), vector(1024)) plus two
    GIN indexes, so the update re-inserted the row into the 1024-d HNSW graph
    for a vector that had not changed. Measured below: 11 s for 5000 chunks,
    against 50 ms for the side table. nexus.chunk_orphaned_at has a primary key
    and nothing else, so an upsert into it touches no vector index. The cost
    of the choice is one extra indexed probe in the predicate.

    The triggers do NOT read the manifest to decide whether the chunk is
    "really" ownerless: two concurrent writers that drop the last two owners of
    a shared chunk each saw the other's uncommitted row and recorded nothing
    (reproduced by ChunkIsReapableIntegrationTest.aConcurrentDropOfASharedChunkLeavesItStamped
    against the first version). Recording every dropped key is safe because
    reapable(c) needs no owner: a recorded chunk that is still owned is simply
    not reapable (condition 2), and one that loses its other owners later has
    its record refreshed.

    LOCKING, AND WHAT IS CLAIMED. Each trigger first runs
        SELECT 1 FROM nexus.chunks c JOIN (the dropped keys) ...
         ORDER BY c.tenant_id, c.collection, c.chash FOR NO KEY UPDATE OF c
    and then upserts. The chunk rows are the rows to lock because a chunk row
    exists for every key that is recorded (the upsert is driven from the chunk
    rows, so a key with no chunk is skipped), it is the one row every writer of
    the key has in common, and it needs no insert, so the lock pass is a single
    ordered walk over rows that already exist. What that buys, and only that:
      - two triggers that share chunks lock them in the same total order, so
        TRIGGER-VERSUS-TRIGGER cannot deadlock on chunk rows, and the second
        waits for the first's commit and then reads its committed orphaned_at,
        so the one-hour guard below sees it and no record is lost;
      - the upsert itself then runs in the same key order (ORDER BY on its
        select), so the side-table rows are also taken in one order.
    It does not claim to order against other writers. The content upserts
    (PgVectorRepository.upsertChunks, CatalogRepository.upsertManifestChunkVectors)
    sort their chunk writes by chash within one (tenant, collection), which
    makes every pass monotone in chash, but no test pins that, and the manifest
    write itself is not under DeadlockRetry. A 40P01 against another writer
    would be loud (an error, not a lost record) and retryable.
    ChunkIsReapableIntegrationTest pins the ORDER BY and the lock clause in both
    function bodies (pg_get_functiondef), the concurrent shared-chunk drop, and
    the record's one-hour guard; the header's no-deadlock claim stops at the
    pinned part.
    Chunk-row locking costs about 12 ms per 5000 chunks (measured: 51 to 60 ms
    with the lock, 39 to 48 ms for the upsert alone) and makes a writer that
    refreshes one of those chunks wait for the manifest transaction, which is
    what the first version's UPDATE did as well.

    SECURITY. The functions are SECURITY INVOKER, as every function in the
    changelog is (an earlier draft made them SECURITY DEFINER only so a test that
    revokes SELECT (chash) from nexus_svc kept passing; not reading the manifest
    removes that need). They run under the writer's FORCE RLS and tenant GUC, and
    join on tenant_id besides, so a tenant-A statement never records a tenant-B
    chunk; EXECUTE is revoked from PUBLIC (a trigger function cannot be called
    directly anyway) and ChunkIsReapableIntegrationTest pins prosecdef = false and
    the ACL. The tenant equality is defence in depth for the service role (RLS
    already binds it) and the ONLY barrier for a role that bypasses RLS, so it has
    its own test, run as the table owner.

    WHAT IT COVERS. Doing this in the database covers every DML path that drops
    an owner row (writeManifestRows' replace, append's ON CONFLICT upsert of a
    position, purgeManifest, delete-collection, the FK cascade of a hard document
    delete, the SQL maintenance functions, the .nxexp import, anything added
    later) instead of a list of Java call sites that rots.

    WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, stated so nobody assumes it does (RDR-192 carries the
    per-path consequence table):
      - TRUNCATE of the manifest: statement DELETE triggers do not fire. No code
        path truncates it, and nexus_svc holds no TRUNCATE on it
        (ChunkIsReapableIntegrationTest pins that with has_table_privilege). Run by
        a superuser it orphans every chunk with no record: REAPED EARLY, and for a
        live chunk REAPED WRONGLY unless a consumer's own gates (census, fraction
        floor, quarantine) catch it.
      - a session with session_replication_role = replica (pg_restore, logical
        apply): triggers do not fire, and neither does the FK cascade. A manual
        replica-mode DELETE is reaped early.
      - manifest DML by a role that is exempt from RLS on the manifest alone: the
        manifest rows are deleted, but the policy on nexus.chunks hides every chunk
        from a role that is subject to it with no tenant GUC, so nothing is
        recorded (by reading the policies; it cannot be built in a test without
        NO FORCE on the manifest alone). A role subject to RLS on both with no GUC
        deletes ZERO manifest rows, so nothing is dropped (pinned). A role exempt
        from both (BYPASSRLS, a superuser, the table owner) records correctly
        (pinned).
      - the one-hour guard (below): up to one hour early.
      - a chunk promoted from staging over an existing row (StagingPromoteOps ON
        CONFLICT DO NOTHING) is not an orphaning and keeps its old clocks, so an
        aged ownerless chunk re-promoted is reapable between the promote and the
        manifest finalize until nexus-z0o2p.27 lands.
    The grace therefore protects a run's old tail for 30 days after batch 1, and a
    deleted file's chunks for 30 days after the purge, on every path that drops a
    manifest row.

    THE ONE-HOUR GUARD. The writer has usually just refreshed the chunks it then
    drops from the manifest (the content upsert runs before the manifest delete in
    the same transaction and sets last_written_at = now()), and such a chunk is
    already inside its grace by 30 days less an hour. So a chunk is recorded only
    when BOTH its last_written_at and its existing orphaned_at (if any) are older
    than one hour: the select filters on last_written_at, the upsert's DO UPDATE
    is conditioned on the existing orphaned_at. A plain "< now()" would skip only
    rows the SAME transaction refreshed; the one-hour margin also skips rows an
    earlier request of the same run refreshed moments ago (the writer and the
    manifest change are often separate requests, hence separate transactions). The
    price: a chunk written within the hour before it loses its owner is reapable
    up to one hour earlier than a recorded chunk would be, 30 days less that hour
    after its write. It is never reapable at once, and never later.

    COST, measured (nexus-wbfpw.15, 2026-10-01; one Docker pgvector 17.11 container
    on an Apple-silicon laptop at load average 10 to 13, so the absolute figures
    carry contention noise and the ratios are the evidence). Table shape: 35,000
    chunks of 1024 dimensions in one collection, about 1.6 KB of text each, all
    three HNSW indexes (m = 16, ef_construction = 64), both GIN indexes and the
    two btrees, the manifest with its foreign key to nexus.chunks, row level
    security forced on all three tables, the statements run by a NOSUPERUSER
    NOBYPASSRLS role with the tenant GUC set, shared_buffers 128 MB. All 5000
    chunks were last written 40 days before; statements rolled back; three runs
    each, range given. "old" is the first version (UPDATE nexus.chunks);
    "new" is this changeset; "off" is the triggers dropped.

      DELETE of 5000 manifest rows, one statement
          off 1-2 ms       old 11.1-13.9 s       new 51-60 ms
      the same, the 5000 chunks refreshed in the same transaction (the guard path)
          off 3 ms         old 39-41 ms          new 43-48 ms
      1000 documents x 5 chunks, 1000 statements (the FK cascade of a hard delete
      fires one statement per document)
          off 14-15 ms     old 11.0-12.1 s       new 77-84 ms
      batch 1 of a multi-batch re-index (5000 old tail chunks dropped), then a later
      batch re-adds and refreshes them, one transaction
          stamp: off 2.5 ms, old 10.9 s, new 44 ms; then the refresh (the client's
          ordinary upsert, 10.5-11.8 s for 5000 rows on this table, the same in
          every column)

    The "old" column is the figure the critique measured independently on a 35k
    row table (17 to 19 s under heavier load; 5.3 s on a 7k row table with
    smaller text). The refresh itself is the client's ordinary chunk upsert and is
    NOT a cost of this change: it is what any re-write of 5000 existing chunks
    costs on this table, and the first version paid it a second time for the
    stamp. The side table's predicate probe adds one primary-key lookup per
    candidate chunk that already passed the manifest and quarantine tests; the
    EXPLAIN of the reaper-shaped statement is in
    ChunkIsReapablePlanIntegrationTest (it asserts chunk_orphaned_at_pk is the
    probe). A 10,000-chunk old tail dropped at batch 1 of a re-index costs about
    0.1 s inside the shared sweep gate and the index-run lock, not the tens of
    seconds the first version cost.

    STALE SIDE ROWS ARE HARMLESS, AND CLEANED. The table cannot hold a row for a
    chunk that no longer exists: the foreign key to nexus.chunks is ON DELETE
    CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE (the topic_assignments precedent, taxonomy-012), so
    deleting a chunk (the reaper, the quarantine move, delete-collection,
    purge_trash) removes its record, and a collection rename or re-home rewrites
    the key. A record whose chunk is still there but is no longer true cannot make
    a chunk reapable wrongly, because the record only ever LATE-bounds the grace:
      - the chunk was re-owned: condition 2 fails, whatever the record says;
      - the chunk was re-written: last_written_at is newer and wins in GREATEST;
      - the chunk lost its owner again: the trigger refreshes the record (its
        existing orphaned_at is older than the hour guard, or the chunk was written
        within the hour and is inside the grace anyway).
    ChunkIsReapableIntegrationTest pins each of these, and the cascade.

    R8: THIS PREDICATE IS NOT "NO LIVE OWNER NEEDS THIS CHUNK". A live legacy
    note stored before nexus-b6enc has a catalog document but no manifest row;
    once its chunk is old it satisfies every condition above and reads
    reapable (RDR-192 S1a matrix row R8). The guard is the manifest-less census
    gate, not the SQL: a consumer that acts on this predicate must first see
    legacy-unmanifested = 0 for the collection, and a periodic consumer must
    re-run that census in the engine on every pass rather than trust a stored
    record (T2 review-wbfpw41-substantive-critique S1). The same text is in
    COMMENT ON FUNCTION below.

    PARAMETERS. p_grace is an interval; NULL means the default (30 days), which
    lives here and nowhere else. Tests inject small values; a delete path must
    clamp a user-supplied grace to a floor before it reaches this function (the
    SQL stays pure). ReapableConsumersScanTest enforces it: every statement that
    deletes or copies chunks under this predicate sits in a function that takes
    the exclusive sweep gate and passes NULL, with the listing route as the named
    exemption.

    SHAPE. Same as live(c) (vectors-018): a set-returning function, because a
    scalar RETURNS boolean whose body holds an EXISTS subquery can never be
    inlined, and an opaque per-row call is what the .9 round-1 EXPLAIN caught.
    It returns one row when the chunk is reapable and none otherwise, so a
    caller writes:

        EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM nexus.chunk_is_reapable(
                    c.tenant_id, c.collection, c.chash, c.last_written_at, NULL))

    The record is read by a scalar subquery on its primary key inside the
    GREATEST, not by a LEFT JOIN. The two return the same rows. A join form was
    measured and its plan depends on the side table's statistics: a freshly
    created, empty table plans a sequential scan with a nested loop, and a
    statement that grows the table while it runs (a cascade of 1000 deletes)
    kept that plan and went quadratic (3.2 s for the 1000 x 5 shape where the
    primary-key form took 80 ms). The subquery is an InitPlan probe on the primary
    key that runs only for a row that passed the cheaper tests.

    The row's own last_written_at is PASSED IN rather than re-read, which is
    what lets the same call be the WHERE of a DELETE: after inlining, the grace
    comparison is a qual on the DELETE's own target row, so a client write that
    refreshes last_written_at while the DELETE waits on its row lock is seen when
    READ COMMITTED rechecks the new row version, and the DELETE skips it. A
    select-then-delete-by-key would not see it. The manifest and record
    sub-queries are not rechecked by that mechanism (they are read in the
    statement's snapshot): a manifest row committed during the DELETE is the sweep
    gate's concern (every destructive consumer takes it exclusively), not this
    function's, and a record written during it can only come from a manifest
    delete, which the same gate serialises against. The function inlines but
    PostgreSQL does not pull the EXISTS up into a semi-join, so it plans as a
    per-row subplan with index probes (idx_catalog_chunks_chash,
    catalog_collections' primary key, chunk_orphaned_at_pk), the same plan live(c)
    gets; ChunkIsReapablePlanIntegrationTest pins that under nexus_svc.

    NOT A VIEW JOIN, for the reason vectors-009 and vectors-018 give.
    LANGUAGE sql, STABLE, SECURITY INVOKER (FORCE RLS on the catalog tables stays
    the isolation boundary), no function-level SET, not STRICT; EXECUTE is the
    PUBLIC default, as for chunk_live_owners.

    NO INDEX is added for last_written_at. A btree on it would stop HOT updates
    for every client re-write of a chunk (nexus-wbfpw.43 review), and the
    candidate scan is already bounded by the (tenant_id, collection, chash)
    primary key prefix. ChunkIsReapablePlanIntegrationTest asserts the schema has
    no such index. The side table is indexed by its primary key alone, which is
    the probe key.

    STATISTICS. The table grows in bursts (a 5000-chunk drop adds 5000 rows in one
    statement) from empty, and the predicate's probe on it is a primary-key lookup
    that the planner only prefers to a sequential scan once it knows the table is
    not tiny. autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor = 0.02 (default 0.1, the
    tuples-007 precedent for a table whose churn is high relative to its size)
    makes autoanalyze fire after a burst rather than after ten times as many rows.
    The planner also reads the table's physical size at plan time, so the window
    in which a plan can be stale is a plan cached in a session before the burst.

    GRANTS AND RLS. nexus.chunk_orphaned_at is ENABLE + FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY
    with the tenant_isolation policy (vectors-004 and tuples-007's shape).
    nexus_svc and nexus_diag pick the table up automatically:
    grants-nexus-svc.xml and grants-nexus-diag.xml iterate every relation in
    schema nexus at execution time and run after this file. The foreign key's
    cascade runs as the table owner and is exempt from RLS, as taxonomy-012's is.

    OTHER PREDICATES. strandedChunkCount (purge_trash Step 1) does NOT move onto
    this function: it counts chunks that HAVE a manifest row whose owners are all
    aged tombstones, the complement of arm 2 above, so the two are disjoint by
    design and together make the dead set. purge_trash keeps its own deleted_at
    grace. RDR-192 Technical Design records the same decision.

    KNOWN CONSEQUENCE AT DEPLOY. vectors-020's ADD COLUMN ... DEFAULT now() gives
    every existing chunk the migration time, so for 30 days after the engine
    carrying vectors-020 is deployed nothing that already exists is reapable.

    ROW TYPES. RETURNS TABLE adds one generated jOOQ record
    (ChunkIsReapableRecord) and the new table adds another
    (ChunkOrphanedAtRecord); JooqRecordReflectionFeatureTest's count moves by +2.
    The new tenant table is also listed in the doctor's RLS canary
    (src/nexus/health.py _RLS_TENANT_TABLES and its mirror in
    tests/test_health_service_checks.py).

    No DATA EFFECT: a table, a function and two triggers; no existing row changes at
    deploy (the table starts empty, and nothing back-fills it, because a chunk that is
    ownerless today has no recorded orphaning time and its grace runs from
    last_written_at, which vectors-020 set to the migration time).
    Placed after vectors-020 and before the grants-nexus-svc.xml runAlways block (D0.1).
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="vectors-021-1" author="nexus-wbfpw.15">
        <comment>nexus.chunk_orphaned_at: when a chunk last lost an owner row (RDR-192 reapable(c)'s grace anchor for ownerless chunks), written by the manifest triggers of vectors-021-3, read by nexus.chunk_is_reapable. Primary key (tenant_id, collection, chash); foreign key to nexus.chunks ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE so a record never outlives or misnames its chunk; RLS ENABLE+FORCE with the tenant_isolation policy. CREATE TABLE only, no data mutation of any existing row, so no DATA EFFECT line applies.</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
CREATE TABLE nexus.chunk_orphaned_at (
    tenant_id   TEXT        NOT NULL,
    collection  TEXT        NOT NULL,
    chash       BYTEA       NOT NULL,
    orphaned_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
    CONSTRAINT chunk_orphaned_at_pk PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, collection, chash),
    CONSTRAINT chunk_orphaned_at_chunk_fk FOREIGN KEY (tenant_id, collection, chash)
        REFERENCES nexus.chunks (tenant_id, collection, chash)
        ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE
) WITH (autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor = 0.02);

ALTER TABLE nexus.chunk_orphaned_at ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE nexus.chunk_orphaned_at FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON nexus.chunk_orphaned_at
    USING      (tenant_id = current_setting('nexus.tenant', true))
    WITH CHECK (tenant_id = current_setting('nexus.tenant', true));
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
DROP POLICY IF EXISTS tenant_isolation ON nexus.chunk_orphaned_at;
ALTER TABLE IF EXISTS nexus.chunk_orphaned_at NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE IF EXISTS nexus.chunk_orphaned_at DISABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS nexus.chunk_orphaned_at;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="vectors-021-2" author="nexus-wbfpw.15">
        <comment>nexus.chunk_is_reapable(tenant, collection, chash, last_written_at, grace): RDR-192 reapable(c) as an inlinable set-returning SQL function. One row when the chunk has no own-collection manifest row in any owner state, the later of last_written_at and its orphaned_at (nexus.chunk_orphaned_at) is older than the grace (default 30 days) and its collection is not a quarantine sibling; no row otherwise. No call site migrated here (Step 8).</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION nexus.chunk_is_reapable(
    p_tenant          text,
    p_collection      text,
    p_chash           bytea,
    p_last_written_at timestamptz,
    p_grace           interval DEFAULT NULL
) RETURNS TABLE(reapable boolean)
LANGUAGE sql
STABLE
SECURITY INVOKER
AS $$
    SELECT true
     WHERE GREATEST(
               p_last_written_at,
               (SELECT o.orphaned_at
                  FROM nexus.chunk_orphaned_at o
                 WHERE o.tenant_id  = p_tenant
                   AND o.collection = p_collection
                   AND o.chash      = p_chash)
           ) &lt; now() - COALESCE(p_grace, interval '30 days')
       AND NOT EXISTS (
             SELECT 1
               FROM nexus.catalog_document_chunks m
              WHERE m.tenant_id  = p_tenant
                AND m.collection = p_collection
                AND m.chash      = p_chash
           )
       AND NOT EXISTS (
             SELECT 1
               FROM nexus.catalog_collections cc
              WHERE cc.tenant_id = p_tenant
                AND cc.name      = p_collection
                AND cc.lifecycle_state = 'quarantine'
           )
$$;

COMMENT ON FUNCTION nexus.chunk_is_reapable(text, text, bytea, timestamptz, interval) IS
    'RDR-192 reapable(c): one row when the chunk has no own-collection manifest row in any owner state, the later of its last_written_at and its orphaned_at is older than the grace (NULL = 30 days) and the collection is not a quarantine sibling. NOT "no live owner needs this chunk": a live legacy note with no manifest row (matrix row R8) reads reapable once aged, so a consumer must first see the manifest-less census read legacy-unmanifested = 0 for the collection, and a periodic consumer must re-run it in the engine on every pass. A destructive caller must take the exclusive sweep gate, pass NULL or a floor-clamped grace, and carry this call in its own DELETE so a racing client write wins. orphaned_at (nexus.chunk_orphaned_at) is written by a trigger whenever a manifest statement drops a chunk key, so the grace is time since the chunk was last written or last lost an owner row.';
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS nexus.chunk_is_reapable(text, text, bytea, timestamptz, interval);</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="vectors-021-3" author="nexus-wbfpw.15">
        <comment>The orphaning record: statement-level AFTER DELETE and AFTER UPDATE triggers on nexus.catalog_document_chunks upsert nexus.chunk_orphaned_at (orphaned_at = now()) for every chunk key a statement dropped from the manifest, in the manifest change's own transaction, after locking those chunk rows in key order (FOR NO KEY UPDATE) and honouring a one-hour guard on both last_written_at and the existing orphaned_at. They never write nexus.chunks (a chunk-row UPDATE re-inserts the row into the 1024-d HNSW index), SECURITY INVOKER, EXECUTE revoked from PUBLIC; they do not read the manifest. Covers every DML path that drops an owner row, so reapable(c)'s grace means time since the chunk became ownerless.</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_delete()
RETURNS trigger
LANGUAGE plpgsql
SECURITY INVOKER
AS $$
BEGIN
    -- Lock the chunk rows first, in key order: two writers that drop the last two owners of a shared
    -- chunk serialize here (the second blocks until the first commits, then reads its committed
    -- orphaned_at), and writers that share several chunks take their locks in the same order, so two
    -- of these triggers cannot deadlock on chunk rows. The key list is the same as the upsert's.
    PERFORM 1
       FROM nexus.chunks c
       JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT tenant_id, collection, chash FROM old_rows) o
         ON o.tenant_id = c.tenant_id AND o.collection = c.collection AND o.chash = c.chash
      WHERE c.last_written_at &lt; now() - interval '1 hour'
      ORDER BY c.tenant_id, c.collection, c.chash
        FOR NO KEY UPDATE OF c;

    -- Record the orphaning. The one-hour guard: a chunk written within the hour is skipped here
    -- (the select filters on last_written_at) and a chunk already recorded within the hour is
    -- skipped by the DO UPDATE's WHERE. Driven from the chunk rows, so a key with no chunk (the
    -- foreign key would refuse it) records nothing.
    INSERT INTO nexus.chunk_orphaned_at AS q (tenant_id, collection, chash, orphaned_at)
    SELECT c.tenant_id, c.collection, c.chash, now()
      FROM nexus.chunks c
      JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT tenant_id, collection, chash FROM old_rows) o
        ON o.tenant_id = c.tenant_id AND o.collection = c.collection AND o.chash = c.chash
     WHERE c.last_written_at &lt; now() - interval '1 hour'
     ORDER BY c.tenant_id, c.collection, c.chash
    ON CONFLICT (tenant_id, collection, chash) DO UPDATE
       SET orphaned_at = EXCLUDED.orphaned_at
     WHERE q.orphaned_at &lt; now() - interval '1 hour';
    RETURN NULL;
END;
$$;

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_update()
RETURNS trigger
LANGUAGE plpgsql
SECURITY INVOKER
AS $$
BEGIN
    -- The keys the statement dropped are the old keys minus the new keys: a row that keeps its
    -- (tenant, collection, chash), whatever else changed, drops nothing; one that moves position and
    -- chash together drops the old chash. Same lock, same upsert, same guard as the DELETE trigger.
    PERFORM 1
       FROM nexus.chunks c
       JOIN (SELECT tenant_id, collection, chash FROM old_rows
             EXCEPT
             SELECT tenant_id, collection, chash FROM new_rows) o
         ON o.tenant_id = c.tenant_id AND o.collection = c.collection AND o.chash = c.chash
      WHERE c.last_written_at &lt; now() - interval '1 hour'
      ORDER BY c.tenant_id, c.collection, c.chash
        FOR NO KEY UPDATE OF c;

    INSERT INTO nexus.chunk_orphaned_at AS q (tenant_id, collection, chash, orphaned_at)
    SELECT c.tenant_id, c.collection, c.chash, now()
      FROM nexus.chunks c
      JOIN (SELECT tenant_id, collection, chash FROM old_rows
            EXCEPT
            SELECT tenant_id, collection, chash FROM new_rows) o
        ON o.tenant_id = c.tenant_id AND o.collection = c.collection AND o.chash = c.chash
     WHERE c.last_written_at &lt; now() - interval '1 hour'
     ORDER BY c.tenant_id, c.collection, c.chash
    ON CONFLICT (tenant_id, collection, chash) DO UPDATE
       SET orphaned_at = EXCLUDED.orphaned_at
     WHERE q.orphaned_at &lt; now() - interval '1 hour';
    RETURN NULL;
END;
$$;

REVOKE EXECUTE ON FUNCTION nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_delete() FROM PUBLIC;
REVOKE EXECUTE ON FUNCTION nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_update() FROM PUBLIC;

CREATE TRIGGER trg_catalog_chunks_stamp_on_delete
    AFTER DELETE ON nexus.catalog_document_chunks
    REFERENCING OLD TABLE AS old_rows
    FOR EACH STATEMENT
    EXECUTE FUNCTION nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_delete();

CREATE TRIGGER trg_catalog_chunks_stamp_on_update
    AFTER UPDATE ON nexus.catalog_document_chunks
    REFERENCING OLD TABLE AS old_rows NEW TABLE AS new_rows
    FOR EACH STATEMENT
    EXECUTE FUNCTION nexus.stamp_chunks_on_manifest_update();
        </sql>
